# Local Museum / Historical Society, Business District.
# Mr. Abernathy: antiquarian rigor blinded by class reverence; scholarly denial of the Old Gods.
NPC: local_historian
LOCATION: business_district
SCHEDULE: morning=museum, midday=museum, evening=museum, night=upper_house_4

TOPIC: default
  GATE: NOT topic_done(local_historian, default) AND coat = plain
  TAG: historian_plain
  MR. ABERNATHY: "Admittance is ten cents, which supports the preservation of maritime relics."
  [The curator peers up from a vitrine filled with tarnished whale-oil lamps and baleen corsetry.]
  MR. ABERNATHY: "Mind the glass on the center case. The hinge is delicate and the county grant does not cover careless elbows."

TOPIC: default
  GATE: NOT topic_done(local_historian, default)
  TAG: historian_badge
  MR. ABERNATHY: "Officer Corwin. If this is regarding the regrettable affair at the Ophion estate, the Historical Society has already prepared our condolence resolution."
  WALTER CORWIN: "I'm looking for history, Mr. Abernathy, not condolences."
  MR. ABERNATHY: "History is what survives grief, Officer. It is rarely in a hurry."

TOPIC: ship_origin
  GATE: always
  TAG: historian_ophion
  LABEL: "Ask about the naming of the whaling ship Ophion"
  TIME: 7
  WALTER CORWIN: "The flagship Ophion. Who named her?"
  MR. ABERNATHY: "Elias Pruitt, 1819. A gentleman of classical discernment, educated at Harvard College."
  [He taps a framed pen-and-ink rendering of the three-masted bark on the wood-paneled wall.]
  MR. ABERNATHY: "In Orphic cosmogony, Ophion was the primordial serpent who ruled Mount Olympus alongside Eurynome before being cast down into Tartarus by Kronos."
  WALTER CORWIN: "A serpent cast down."
  MR. ABERNATHY: "A respectable classical flourish, Officer! It was fashionable among Federalist merchant families to bestow Greek and Roman names upon commercial ventures. Nothing more sinister than an educated man's literary vanity."
  WALTER CORWIN: "The club kept the name."
  MR. ABERNATHY: "To honor their forebears' enterprise. Though I confess... Dr. Fenn and Judge Wexford began collecting later theological interpretations that modern scholarship finds quite absurd."
  NOTEBOOK: historian_ophion_1 | "Historian explains the Ophion was named in 1819 from Orphic cosmogony (primordial serpent overthrown); insists it was mere classical vanity by merchants."
  EVIDENCE: ophion_myth_classical

TOPIC: dr_fenn_library
  GATE: evidence(ophion_myth_classical)
  TAG: historian_fenn_books
  LABEL: "Ask what Dr. Fenn was researching in the archives"
  TIME: 6
  MR. ABERNATHY: "Aldous Fenn spent weeks in our basement folio collection two winters ago."
  WALTER CORWIN: "What was he looking for?"
  MR. ABERNATHY: "Pre-Colonial shoreline surveys. And translations of Aegean mystery rites.\nHe brought in a battered German volume on Near Eastern chthonic cults."
  FORK:
    CHOICE: "Ask about the annotations."
      MR. ABERNATHY: "He was manic with his pencil. Underlining passages about gods that were not slain, only 'displaced.'\nI told him he was reading modern occultist rubbish into straightforward Athenian allegory, but a wealthy doctor does not welcome correction from a curator."
      NOTEBOOK: historian_fenn_annotations | "Fenn studied Aegean mystery rites, obsessively annotating passages regarding gods 'displaced rather than slain.'"
      EVIDENCE: displaced_god_doctrine
    CHOICE: "Ask what Fenn took from the collection."
      MR. ABERNATHY: "A survey map drawn by the try-works overseer in 1822, showing subterranean drainage conduits beneath the kitchen bluff.\nHe never returned the plate. Claimed it was lost to damp."
      NOTEBOOK: historian_fenn_map | "Dr. Fenn borrowed and never returned an 1822 survey showing underground drainage conduits beneath the estate's kitchen bluff."
      EVIDENCE: estate_conduit_map

TOPIC: the_free_crew
  GATE: evidence(lay_lead) AND NOT topic_done(local_historian, the_free_crew)
  TAG: historian_crew_lineage
  LABEL: "Ask about free Black whalemen aboard the 1823 voyage"
  TIME: 5
  WALTER CORWIN: "The Ophion's crew list carries the name Freeman. Were they transients?"
  MR. ABERNATHY: "Transients? Great heavens, no."
  [He removes his spectacles, cleaning them with a silk handkerchief.]
  MR. ABERNATHY: "Massachusetts abolished slavery through judicial decree in the 1780s, Officer. Coastal maritime communities had free families of color established for generations before the Ophion ever tasted salt.\nThe Freemans were skilled harpooners and boat-steerers. Their lineage in this commonwealth predates the Pruitt mills by forty years."
  WALTER CORWIN: "Why did the town registry call Naomi a transient?"
  MR. ABERNATHY: "Because a town registry is written by living clerks to serve living landowners, Officer.\nPaper forgets whatever it is inconvenient to remember."
  NOTEBOOK: historian_crew_1 | "Historian confirms the Freeman whaling lineage was free by Massachusetts law since 1780s; calls town's 'transient' label a living clerk's convenience."
  EVIDENCE: freeman_lineage_free
